  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe upper level ridge across the Central CONUS continues to build as \na series of shortwaves move around the northern periphery through \nthe middle of the week. Firstly, there is a slight chance for \ndiurnal showers and thunderstorms this afternoon into the\nevening, though coverage should be minimal. Late tonight into \nearly Monday morning, the first of the shortwaves will move just\nwest of the region and precipitation chances will increase. \nCurrent guidance has an MCS tracking along a thermal gradient \nthat is situated northwest to southeast across Indiana. Recent \ntrends have this feature not impacting northwestern Ohio until \nMonday morning, if the precipitation does reach this far east. \nAny storms that do reach the area have the potential for strong \nto severe winds, and as a result, much of the region is under a \nMarginal Risk (level 1 out of 5) for severe weather on SPC's Day\n1 Severe Weather Outlook. \n\nThere will be a lull in precipitation into the afternoon on Monday \nbefore the second shortwave ahead of a surface low pressure tracks \nthrough the Great Lakes region to the southeast. The upper level \nridge, and thermal gradient will have shifted to the east a little \nmore as well. Out ahead of the surface low, bulk shear vectors will \nbe out of the northwest at 40-50 knots that will support strong to \nsevere thunderstorms Monday evening. As the front approaches late \nMonday night, storms will develop into a more linear mode. Damaging \nwinds will be the main threat throughout the event, especially with \nthe linear mode, but can't rule out any large hail given the \nstrength of the deep layer shear and instability in any cells \ndeveloping ahead of the line. There is still some uncertainty given \nhow the 12Z models have handled this feature, with the HRRR keeping \nit well to the west and the NAM Nest developing it across central \nMichigan and moving into northern Ohio. In addition, how long \nprecipitation lingers through the morning and coverage of any late \nevening storms may inhibit storm development with the front. Highest \nconfidence currently is in northwestern Ohio to Cleveland, south\nthrough the Mansfield area for damaging wind impacts. SPC\ncurrently has the entire area in a Slight Risk (level 2 out of \n5) for severe weather with the main driver being the damaging \nwind threat. \n\nBehind the cold front Tuesday morning, a surface trough will linger \nacross the eastern portion of the region with strong northerly flow \nexpected. Residual moisture is expected, primarily across \nNortheastern Ohio and Northwestern Pennsylvania that will aid in \nlake effect showers developing. Periodic showers are possible across \nthe aforementioned area through at least Wednesday when drier air \nmoves into the region from the west with high pressure.",
